# Build output.
#
# These patterns are anchored with a leading slash on purpose. An unanchored
# "flight-recorder" would also match the directories cmd/flight-recorder and
# deploy/helm/flight-recorder, silently excluding the entire CLI and Helm chart
# from the repository.
/bin/
/dist/
*.exe
/flight-recorder
/flight-recorder-linux-*

# Coverage and profiling
coverage.out
coverage.html
*.prof
*.pprof
*.test

# SBOM
sbom.cdx.json
sbom.spdx.json

# Evidence and keys must never be committed. A memory dump routinely contains
# credentials and personal data; a signing key would let anyone forge evidence.
/evidence/
*.pem
*.key
dump.bin*
receipt.json
metadata.jsonld
manifest.json
audit.jsonl

# Local scratch
/tmp/
*.local
.env

# Editors
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store

# Note on generated eBPF artefacts: pkg/probe/recorder_bpfel.go and
# recorder_bpfel.o ARE committed on purpose, so that `go build` works without a
# clang toolchain. Only regenerate them with `make generate`.

# The one deliberate exception to the rules above: examples/ carries a bundle
# produced by `flight-recorder demo`, whose payload is synthetic and contains
# no real memory, plus the PUBLIC half of the throwaway key that signed it.
# It exists so that anyone can run `verify` against real output without
# building the tool first.
#
# These are listed file by file rather than with a blanket !examples/** so
# that a private key dropped into this directory stays ignored. The build
# workflow additionally fails if any private key material is ever committed.
!/examples/demo-pub.pem
!/examples/demo-bundle/manifest.json
!/examples/demo-bundle/metadata.jsonld
!/examples/demo-bundle/receipt.json
!/examples/demo-bundle/regions.json
!/examples/demo-bundle/dump.bin.sha256
!/examples/demo-bundle/dump.bin.zst.enc
